Z||000005||Alex4mula||06-01-2001||01:32 PM||||Thanks guys. I made the mold out of green floral foam. I hot glued several blocks & cut the basic square shape (dim E,D,F) then test fitted several times leaving clearance for glass thickness. Then added bottom part (dim A,B,C) and test fitted again. Added a 3/4" MDF plate for the sub face (hole pre-cut) and glued it to the foam mold. Then I taped the mold (foam part) with masking tape. Over that I put 4 layers of fiberglass (about 3/16" to 1/4" thick). Would have added 2 more but I was lazy and wanted to finish it. After all was dry I took out the foam in chunks. Easy to do with a knife or whatever. Then I was also able to take out the masking tape out of the walls too (not important). I added some small wood bracing to the walls on the top portion for added rigidity. Added poly foam, carpet & installed an 8" RF XLC. With my 120W sub amp sounds extremely good to me.<BR>Fit is very snug. It is being held by couple of black tie-raps. no movement at all. You can tailor that. Doing another for sale??? Sorry but no way. Working with FG is very messy. Was my first time but not last. May do another for my future Fiero. Will see...||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||06-01-2001||02:08 PM||||Looks good! Something else to add to my list...||||reg||14
Z||000007||Oreif||06-01-2001||02:21 PM||||So going by the external dimensions and calculating the internal dimensions, (3/4" MDF on bottom and 1/4" on fiberglass walls but not taking into account the wood brace) The volume would be:<BR>A,B, & C you have an internal volume of .102 cubic feet.<BR>D, E, & F has an internal volume of .173 cubic feet.<BR>That is a total of .275 cubic feet.<BR>Subtract .08 (speaker displacement) from the total volume leaves .195 cubic feet of volume.<BR>Minimum required for an 8" RF XLC sub is .3 cubic feet.  That is 33% less volume than required. So the VAS of the speaker is .636 minus the 33% (.209) loss of suspension volume leaves .427 Which decreases cone extesion from .31" to .22". So the lowest frequency the sub could produce with a cone surface ares of 34.1" squared at .22" of displacement is 59.673hz with a roll-off of 24db. At 24db roll-off and 85db of speaker sensitivity, the lowest frequency possible out of the sub at 3db volume level (barely audiable) is 3 octives lower or 51.5 hz. The speaker in the correct size box has a low end of 34hz at 12db. A standard aftermarket 4X10 goes down to 50hz at 6db roll-off.<P>Just thought you'd like to know.<P>(RF XLC 8" spec's taken from RF's website)<P>Oops,  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">  math error corrected (thanks FieroGT88) <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Alex4mula||06-01-2001||03:05 PM||||Oreif, I think you need to check you math a little bit. Also, If you think a 4x9 will give you the bass this thing gives me man you are smoking something good. I appreciate your technical jargon but in this case theory and reality doesn't match. The box works pretty well & I can show it to anybody. This is just another option. If you don't believe it then sorry. Just thought you'd like to know.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fierogt88||06-01-2001||04:19 PM||||.275 minus .08 is .195, not .095, over double the "33%" volume that was used in the calculations.  Just a rough head calculation, but it looks like you have nearly 80% of the area "required", which should be pretty good.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||06-01-2001||07:10 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Alex4mula:<BR><B>Oreif, I think you need to check you math a little bit. Also, If you think a 4x9 will give you the bass this thing gives me man you are smoking something good. I appreciate your technical jargon but in this case theory and reality doesn't match. The box works pretty well & I can show it to anybody. This is just another option. If you don't believe it then sorry. Just thought you'd like to know.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> Thanks to FieroGT88, I have corrected the math. (I used the loss, sorry) As for theory and reality not matching, you are incorrect. I bet if you built a box to the correct volume and placed it in the car then switched between the 2 of them you would hear a large difference. As for the "same bass" I was just stating that a 4X10 can produce a lower frequency then the sub. With the corrected math, they are about equal. The 8" will drive (sound pressure level) those frequencies a whole lot better due to air displacement and round speakers are acoustically better than any oval speaker, but you are still losing almost 40hz of low end sound. For acoustical accuracy you will have problems reproducing bass guitars in the music. (very few types of music do not use a bass guitar) Also the decreased air volume will increase pressure and put a strain on the foam suspension of the speaker (weakest point of the speaker) and the cone will eventually seperate. This isn't theory, it's reality. Losing 1/3 (33%) of your internal volume is not good, ask anyone who builds sub boxes.<P>I never said your box was bad. It's actually one of the better designs for under the dash that I've seen. With a Boston Acoustic 6 1/2" sub the box is near perfect. As for how it sounds, your the only one who has to like it. I prefer acoustic accuracy not a "thump mobile". <P>I was just letting you know that the volume of your box was less than recommended. You were the one who told me in the other thread that I was wrong about the size of the sub box that would fit under the dash. You said it was .37 cubic feet (without speaker) and you tested it. Then when I said .37 was impossible in the space of the dash + 2" down, You again stated that I have not seen the diagram and don't know what I'm talking about. Well from your diagram above, you were the one who is incorrect. <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||AusFiero||06-01-2001||08:36 PM||||I notice no one has been attatching their sub boxes to the car real well.<P>Has anybody seen the result of a rollover in a car with something the size and weight of a sub box getting thrown around inside with the passenger and driver? <P>Don't think it can't happen to you as a chunk that size and weight not properly secured is an accident waiting to happen. Probably not even legal in most states and I know it would be laughed at here by registration authorities.<P>Fine, different locations and styles is great, it keeps some inventiveness happening, but for your own sake put more thought into how you mount these things into your car as death by sub woofer would end up on the darwinawards.com immortalising you as one of the worlds dumbest. Do any of us want that to happen?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Urchin||06-01-2001||08:43 PM||||Mine is a similar setup to that, and I just though I'd suggest putting one of those plastic cages over the face (I don't know the technical name).  I've had a few peopl kick mine by accident.<P>------------------<BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_8.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||SPDFREK||06-01-2001||11:56 PM||||speaker grill is the name, but with a Fiero you really don't have many areas to place subs. I ended up taking measurements for the area behind the seats for mine, (am halfway through building them) only problem with my setup is now where ya gonna put the amps? only 3 places, 1)front trunk, 2)rear trunk, 3) under seat. problems with these places, 1) spare and heat from radiator., 2) no air flow for cooling and heat, 3) Fans suck in to much dirt from feet (if equipped with cooling fans). was thinking bout putting the amps in the rear window mounted on a Plexiglas plate mounted to the roof and fire wall. but Don't know how sturdy that would be. anyone have any Ideas for the amps a crossovers?<P>PS, I saw a picture of a sub mounted in the center glove box, might be an idea?!<P>PS. box looks good, but I would try to mount it a little better incase an accident happens.<BR>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/4turbo.gif">       <BR>'88 Fiero 4cycl Turbo,<BR>'88 Fiero GT,<BR>'72 Nova 383 stroker<p>[This message has been edited by SPDFREK (edited 06-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Oreif||06-02-2001||12:15 AM||||SPDFREK, What size subs are you using? Depending on the size you might be able to put them near the top of the firewall behind the seat. Like build them into the top of the subwoofer box. Depending on size of the crossover(s) and how you are wiring the system, You could mount the crossover on the passenger side of the console where the factory sub amp goes. <BR>I have yet to hear a center console subwoofer. AusFiero has a console that looks nice and does not require moving the ECM. I've seen other versions that look good but you need to move the ECM behind one of the seats. The console ones are usually set-up for one 10" sub. I know someone who is going that way (console mounted) so once it is installed we'll hear how it sounds.||||reg||1
