Z||000017||spideyfoot||06-02-2001||01:51 AM||||I say make all the thump you want. I used to have a sonic snob live next door. I thought his fancy Mylar flat speakers sounded like crap and he said my Cerwin Vegas sound like mud. As a bass player I know how they reproduce my sound live that's thump and you know my Ampeg 8x10 cabinet is muddy sounding too and I like it.  I think your sub looks pretty cool and I am inspired to try one. THANKS for sharing it with us. And for the naysayers don't make you one. I hate it when I work hard on something just to be criticized. Maybe they can try the 4x10 with the bass suppository. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||AusFiero||06-02-2001||04:05 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>AusFiero, I think if you manage to roll a Fiero, you have more problems then a sub box hitting you on the head....<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh that I don't doubt, but why create another one. It may be the one that kills you. Have seen many people walk away from rollovers, but not when hit in the head by 20kgs flying around inside the car.<P>Yep Oreif mine does not relocate the ECM. Have finally got the kits to a quality I want after a few hundred bucks experimenting with different joinerys and manufacturing methods. Hope I sell more than the 5 on order though or I have made a nice loss. I need the profit to continue developing body kit stage 2. .35 .45 .5 .6 cubic feet are the sizes available. Can email me or search for sub woofer console on the mall.<P>Shameless plug I know but I havent gone as fare as using my signature file as a pictorial advertisement....YET   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>(Damn it I have. Stage 2 body kit is on it) Oh well, the beauty of editting your own messages before being called a hypocrite hey.<P>Oh yeah speaker pods are ready for shipping too when my credit card machine arrives this week.<P><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.<p>[This message has been edited by AusFiero (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||Alex4mula||06-02-2001||09:24 AM||||Oreif; First lets clarify something. The drawing dimensions are for the mold. So that's why your math is incorrect. Except for the sub plate (Dim A-.75) the rest of the dimensions are internal. That gives these volumes: DEF=.56x.71x.52=0.20, ABC=.81x.75x.21=0.127 for a total volume of 0.33, minus basket  0.08 gives 0.25 which is 25% smaller not 33%. The poly foam inside can compensate for something of this. There is still space up there under the dash to get a total of 0.3cf or more if you want but that would require some odd shape on top of the box which is time consuming to build or hard to install.<BR>Don't get me wrong here. I appreciate your comments and I know you are very knowlegeable in this area. Is just that the way you express gives the impresion that the box will sound like crap when that's not the case at all. There will be some compromises but for the average (& more) people it will work perfectly.<P>Ausi; I agree with you that the box needs to be attached in a better way. I have to work on that. There are several ways. I have seen Vette guys been killed by the targa top flying to their head while stored inside their cars. Also I plan to put a speaker grill. I get very nervious every time my wife or other gets in the car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000020||Oreif||06-02-2001||10:13 AM||||I don't believe that the dimensions are internal size. Using photoshop 5 I scaled out the RF speaker in your pic. Which is 8 3/16" wide. The picture clearly shows the bottom plate as 9.75" wide. Also I just measured in my car. Unless you physically moved the duct/heater core assembly, you cannot fit a 9" width in there. (8.5" internal with 1/4" fiberglass shell) It's snug with an 8.5" width. My internal calculations were done without knowing what the "wood brace on the top" dimensions are too. Even so it's a far cry from .37 cubic feet you claimed before. The poly foam does not compensate for lack of internal volume.<P> I never said it would sound like crap, I said you are losing up to 1/2 of your sub frequencies. (depends on where you have your sub crossover set at). Most "average" people like to have those frequencies.<BR>It goes along the lines of what you don't hear, you don't miss.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||SPDFREK||06-02-2001||09:11 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>SPDFREK, What size subs are you using? Depending on the size you might be able to put them near the top of the firewall behind the seat. Like build them into the top of the subwoofer box. Depending on size of the crossover(s) and how you are wiring the system, You could mount the crossover on the passenger side of the console where the factory sub amp goes. <BR>I have yet to hear a center console subwoofer. AusFiero has a console that looks nice and does not require moving the ECM. I've seen other versions that look good but you need to move the ECM behind one of the seats. The console ones are usually set-up for one 10" sub. I know someone who is going that way (console mounted) so once it is installed we'll hear how it sounds.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>1) I am using 4 10" Punch HX2's, I have them mounted to the firewall behind the 2 seats now, takes up the whole wall. <BR>2) never seen a factory sub system, so I dont know where the amp goes, but I am using a 5 channel (2 front, 2 rear, 1 sub) Crossover, 2 1000 watt amps, 1 600 watt amp, and a cap. Yeah, I saw the center console sub, but was a little worried bout the sound, was thinking of extending the console to go to the roof, and put 2 subs there. Only thing is, I think that would look ugly, not sure though. Let me know what it sounds like when ya hear it though Please.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/test_2.gif">  <P>'88 Fiero 4cycl Turbo,<BR>'88 Fiero GT,<BR>'72 Nova 383 stroker<p>[This message has been edited by SPDFREK (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||Alex4mula||06-02-2001||10:28 PM||||Don't beleive then  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000023||Oreif||06-02-2001||11:31 PM||||SPDFREK, The stock sub amp location isn't big enough for the amp you are using. But one idea is to mount the amp under the passenger side dash on the bottom of the vent housing, all the way in the back where the center tunnel meets the front firewall. I use to have a 6-disc CD changer mounted there. It can't be seen from the outside of the car and won't obstruct someone feet.||||reg||1
Z||000024||SPDFREK||06-04-2001||12:37 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>SPDFREK, The stock sub amp location isn't big enough for the amp you are using. But one idea is to mount the amp under the passenger side dash on the bottom of the vent housing, all the way in the back where the center tunnel meets the front firewall. I use to have a 6-disc CD changer mounted there. It can't be seen from the outside of the car and won't obstruct someone feet.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>GOOD IDEA!!! I wouldn't have thought about there. THX  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> now I gotta get crackin on that!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sig6.jpg"> <BR>'88 Fiero 4cycl Turbo,<BR>'88 Fiero GT,<BR>'72 Nova 383 stroker||||reg||1
